        "summary":  "This article examines AI-powered companion robots like Moxie, marketed to help neurodivergent children develop social skills, and the significant problems that emerge when companies shut down ΓÇö leaving children attached to now-defunct devices. While research shows promise for robotic therapy, the piece highlights limited clinical evidence, data privacy concerns, unpredictable AI behavior, and the ethical crisis of creating emotional dependencies on commercial AI products.",
        "execSummary":  "The Moxie case is a high-visibility instance of a systemic risk that applies well beyond consumer robotics: enterprises and institutions are building operational dependencies on AI systems without adequate continuity planning or data stewardship frameworks. When a deployed AI agent becomes embedded in a workflow or customer relationship and the vendor changes terms, shuts down, or the model is deprecated, the disruption cost is real and often unplanned. The article implicitly argues for a duty-of-care standard for AI products that create dependency, which is beginning to surface in regulatory discussions. The strategic implication for procurement and vendor management is to require continuity and data portability provisions in any AI contract where operational or customer dependency will develop.",
