        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "MIT Technology Review interviewed young people aged 10-18 about their attitudes toward AI, discovering nuanced perspectives rather than simple enthusiasm or rejection. The majority of teens use AI for practical purposes like schoolwork and information searches, though they maintain clear boundaries about what they will not delegate to machines. Notably, kids prioritize concerns about societal impact over personal job displacement and want to preserve creative and critical thinking for themselves.",
        "execSummary":  "The next generation of workers is entering the workforce with a pragmatic but boundary-conscious relationship with AI ΓÇö they use it instrumentally but resist delegating judgment, creativity, and critical thinking. For enterprise AI strategy, this is a leading indicator: future employees will expect AI to augment, not replace, their cognitive work, and cultures that deploy AI in ways that eliminate human judgment will face talent friction. The finding that young people prioritize societal impact concerns over personal job risk suggests that enterprise AI programs will be scrutinized on fairness and broader impact grounds by the workforce they are recruiting. The strategic implication is that AI governance narratives need to be authentic and externally credible, not just internal policy documents.",
